\section{System Architecture and Build Specification}
\label{sec:system-architecture}

\subsection{Overview}
This chapter presents the complete system-level architecture and build
specification for the AFXE-v1.0 engine. The design integrates the hydrodynamic
kernel, entropy extraction substrate, chaotic cryptographic lattice, and
hardware-level sovereignty controller into a unified, production-ready
computational framework. All components adhere to zero-copy memory semantics,
real-time execution constraints, and strict separation of simulation and
cryptographic responsibilities.

\subsection{Repository Structure}
The AFXE engine is organized into a modular directory hierarchy to ensure
clarity of interface boundaries, reproducibility of builds, and isolation of
security-critical components. The canonical structure is:
\begin{verbatim}
afxe_engine/
├── CMakeLists.txt
├── include/
│   ├── afxe_core.cuh
│   └── afxe_hardware_controller.hpp
├── src/
│   ├── afxe_core.cu
│   └── afxe_hardware_controller.cpp
└── scripts/
    └── encryption_lattice.py
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Architectural Layering}
The system is partitioned into four sovereign layers:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Hydrodynamic Core Layer (HDK)}  
      Implements the compressible Navier--Stokes solver, turbulence closure,
      and thermodynamic entropy extraction. This layer is responsible for
      evolving the physical state of the fluid domain.

\item \textbf{Entropy Substrate Layer (ESL)}  
      Aggregates kinetic energy, enstrophy, and thermal contributions into a
      global entropy scalar. This scalar forms the coupling term for the
      chaotic cryptographic lattice.

\item \textbf{Chaotic Cryptographic Lattice (CCL)}  
      Evolves the lattice state using a perturbed tent map. The lattice
      produces high-dimensional cipher masks driven by the physical entropy
      substrate.

\item \textbf{Hardware Sovereignty Controller (HSC)}  
      Enforces zero-copy ingestion, memory residency, and deterministic
      lattice-state evolution within hardware boundaries. This layer ensures
      that cryptographic evolution cannot be decoupled from physical telemetry.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Interface Specification}
The system exposes two primary interfaces:

\paragraph{Hydrodynamic Interface}
The HDK layer provides a single entry point for simulation evolution:
\[
\mathrm{HDK\_Step} : \mathbf{U}_n \mapsto \mathbf{U}_{n+1},
\]
where $\mathbf{U}_n$ denotes the conservative variable vector at time step $n$.
The interface guarantees:
\begin{itemize}
\item bounded residual norms,
\item second-order spatial convergence,
\item deterministic entropy extraction.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Cryptographic Lattice Interface}
The CCL layer exposes a lattice evolution operator:
\[
\mathrm{CCL\_Evolve}(x_n, \bar{E}_n) = x_{n+1},
\]
where $x_n$ is the lattice state and $\bar{E}_n$ is the global entropy scalar.
The operator satisfies:
\begin{itemize}
\item positive Lyapunov exponent ($\lambda > \ln 2$),
\item topological mixing,
\item strict boundedness under perturbation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Build Specification}
The build system is defined using a modern CMake configuration supporting
C++20 and CUDA 12.x. The specification enforces:
\begin{itemize}
\item separable compilation of device kernels,
\item static library generation for the HDK layer,
\item isolated executable generation for the HSC layer,
\item strict include-boundary separation between layers.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Execution Workflow}
The canonical execution workflow consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
\item configuration of the build environment,
\item compilation of the hydrodynamic core,
\item linking of the sovereignty controller,
\item initialization of the entropy substrate,
\item real-time evolution of the coupled PDE--chaotic system.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Sovereign Boundary Guarantee}
\begin{theorem}[Sovereign Execution Boundary]
Let $\mathbf{E}_n$ denote the entropy telemetry buffer at epoch $n$ and
$x_n$ the lattice state. Under zero-copy ingestion and hardware-resident
lattice evolution, no external process can reconstruct $(x_n, x_{n+1})$ or
$\mathbf{E}_n$ without direct access to the hardware controller. Therefore,
the cryptographic evolution is sovereign and non-replicable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The entropy substrate is derived from the physical state of the fluid domain,
which is not externally observable. The hardware controller enforces memory
residency and prohibits disk-based intermediates. The chaotic lattice is
expansive, ensuring that any perturbation or missing entropy sample results in
irreversible divergence. Thus, reconstruction is impossible.
\end{proof}

